

  The HP-UX port provides these meters:

    - load meter
    - cpu meter:  user | nice | system | interrupt | idle
    - mem meter:  text | used | other | free
    - swap meter: used | free
    - page meter: in | out | idle

  All of them read the pstat(2) interface, so they need no privileges.

  Building
  --------

    make

  9.x builds the hpux9 target, 10.x hpux10 and 11.x hpux11.  Name one to
  override: "make hpux9".

  The bundled HP-UX make is enough; there is no need for gmake.

  HP-UX 11 notes
  --------------

  The compiler is the HP ANSI C compiler at /opt/ansic/bin/cc, which ships
  alongside aCC; the bundled /usr/bin/cc is K&R only and will not do.  Say
  "make hpux11 CC=gcc" where that is installed instead.  -Ae turns on the
  ANSI mode the system headers want.  10.20 has the compiler at the same
  path.  9.x is the exception: its target force includes a compatibility
  header, which HP cc cannot do, so it builds with gcc.

  HP-UX ships no libXpm, so the target builds with -DNO_XPM, and it keeps
  X11R6 off the default linker path.

  HP-UX 9 notes
  -------------

  9.x has libc routines that its headers never declare, and no usleep at
  all.  hpux/hpux9/compat.h is force included into every translation unit
  to bridge the gap: it declares snprintf and vsnprintf, and declares a
  usleep that hpux/hpux9/compat.c supplies as a select(2) on no
  descriptors.

  X11R5 lives in /usr/include/X11R5 and /usr/lib/X11R5 rather than on the
  default search paths, and the linker makes a single pass over archives,
  so the target file adds those paths and repeats -lX11 after -lXpm.

  The swap meter is the one meter whose statistic moved: 9.x has no
  pss_nblksenabled and keeps the size of a block device pool and of a file
  system pool in a union instead.
